	"nodes":[
		{"id":"1dcc823cdccf0696","type":"file","file":"Do Not Publish/Drafts/Collective Directionality.md","x":-520,"y":-300,"width":680,"height":2280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"a57e010ff850e49a","type":"text","text":"Pluralistic interpretations of reality","x":220,"y":-180,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"244862401a246c27","type":"text","text":"thick data or warm data","x":220,"y":-80,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5f83621c3c261757","type":"text","text":"“This smart form of domination constantly asks us to communicate our opinions, needs and preferences, to tell our lives, to post, share and like messages,” [writes Han](https://www.theguardian.com/books/2024/feb/18/the-crisis-of-narration-byung-chul-han-review).","x":220,"y":20,"width":400,"height":120},
		{"id":"9a97a64270a1b006","type":"text","text":"We deploy heart-rate data from Fitbits to tell yawnsome just-so stories about fitness journeys; we embellish the tale of what we did on our holidays with selfies and soft-porn snaps of the meal we had at that cute bar we found, according to the permissible parameters of human leisure time, in Oslo. Something has gone missing in all these stories: our individuality, our humanity, our ability to tell convincing narratives rather than perform ourselves.","x":220,"y":160,"width":400,"height":300},
		{"id":"e512b37ec0c62395","type":"text","text":"specially when implementing enforced restrictions, such as creating certain kinds of weapons or using data in certain ways).","x":-860,"y":100,"width":250,"height":160},
		{"id":"46234c0472a2f3cb","type":"text","text":"understand the world and act as a collective organism. but isnt understanding the world about knowledge and we just said it wasn't about knowledge production? Well, its because we","x":-860,"y":280,"width":250,"height":240},
		{"id":"ba2e790b896394f1","type":"text","text":"> \"Wisdom is knowledge of how to live well, and the disposition to act appropriately on that knowledge... Being wise, therefore, means knowing what leads to or constitutes human wellbeing, and being motivated to pursue it.\" [What is Wisdom?](https://practicalphilosopher.prinzing.net/what-is-wisdom-7c9fcb2a3a9c)\n\nmore-than-human wellbeing, being able rather than just motivated","x":-860,"y":-340,"width":250,"height":420},
		{"id":"a799f226f19cb652","type":"text","text":"Essentially, it comes down to how we don't just understand the world through knowledge and facts, as we are becoming increasignly aware of in our post-truth world. \n\n\n\nBruner proposed that there are two distinct forms of thinking: paradigmatic and narrative. Third form: embodied thinking/embodied action as a thinking process. (we'll come back to this third one in more detail later)\n\nhomo narrans, we make sense of the world through narrative. so being more rational is not the (only) answer.","x":-980,"y":560,"width":370,"height":440},
		{"id":"47535db3aec34708","type":"text","text":"When we talk about intentional collective action, such as in an organization or team, often a circular framework such as the OODA loop or feedback loop emerges. Does this really describe the behaviour of complex systems? Cybernetics, not the only way to understand...","x":-1140,"y":-340,"width":250,"height":340},
		{"id":"2bb58ecf45a28184","type":"text","text":"when we talk about better decision making processes, we often talk about better deliberation, more collective participation. Issue-based voting and tools like Polis and Deliberation Garden ???. This is important, because xyz. However, its not the be all and end all. \n\nOften cite Brexit as an example of bad deliberation. ==one paragraph case study here, the usual conclusion is that the media and a few politicians were at fault==\n\nSwitzerland: recently voted to not protect forests because it might be bad for the economy. ==one paragraph case study here, was it bad deliberation or something else?==\n\nImplementation of formal policies is usually only possible after social change or some innovation has taken place. They tend to fit within the paradigm, whether dominant or emergent.\n\nIn the Brexit example, there was a growing narrative about discontent with the economy that was attributed to immigrants, as to the EU by extension. \"Independence\" was seen as a solution to this problem. The media and politicians amplified this narrative to suit their ends.\n\nIn Switzrland, also a narrative of separation from the environment is present, so the economy is seen to take priority event though the economy cant survive without the environment.\n\nThis is leading people to say that we need to \"change the narrative.\" But you cant impose narratives on people, they bubble up, the emerge from interactions and interpretation of those interactions.","x":-1020,"y":1040,"width":459,"height":925},
		{"id":"088a3084973a3f88","type":"text","text":"memes and memecoins as an attempt of some of this","x":220,"y":-280,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5744794e62cb64da","type":"text","text":"symbiotic networks: uniting across silos through action","x":220,"y":-440,"width":250,"height":100},
