		{"id":"e32b6c2696ae44fb","type":"text","text":"what is the relationship between stories and capital, both in the case of storyselling and of narratives?\nAds surface narratives to get you to buy stuff, amplify narratives that serve capital","x":585,"y":-600,"width":250,"height":250},
		{"id":"1aee79ec425ddacb","type":"text","text":"Bruner proposed that there are two distinct forms of thinking: paradigmatic and narrative. Third form: embodied thinking/embodied action as a thinking process?\n\nor cognitive vs non-cognitive decision-making (Snowden)\n\nReaching consensus vs managing disagreement\n\nMany conversations around collective action focus on knowledge production, deliberation and decision making.\n\nSwitzerland: recently voted to not protect forests because it might be bad for the economy.\n\nIndividual as an atom, the collective as a bunch of individuals\n","x":220,"y":640,"width":420,"height":480},
		{"id":"3786b87bb7b09f5f","type":"text","text":"Dave Snowden, creator of the Cynefin framework and one of the founding fathers of human complexity science, has some interesting insights to offer on the topic.\n\nHe argues that although humans have a high capacity for cognitive thought, human social systems are mostly non-cognitive in nature. This means that most of the \"thinking\" happens through the movement of the collective social body.\n\nHis work focuses on narrative as the guiding framework for social movement, and how that translates into micro action that bring about change in the right direction.\n\n\n\n\n\nMove away from the things we don't want, and increase the variety of the things we want.","x":1180,"y":1300,"width":500,"height":1700},
		{"id":"86fb403030f9cde4","type":"file","file":"6 🍂 Compost/Dave Snowden.md","x":1720,"y":1300,"width":500,"height":3460},
		{"id":"425aa0551e21a1a3","type":"link","url":"https://aaronzlewis.com/blog/2019/07/25/metaphors-we-believe-by/","x":3780,"y":140,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"d488c058074cfb7f","type":"link","url":"https://theconvivialsociety.substack.com/p/narrative-collapse-an-addendum","x":3340,"y":280,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"b663b6080dd9a102","type":"link","url":"https://www.boundaryless.io/podcast/dave-snowden/","x":3820,"y":580,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"d91df1d40cd60253","type":"link","url":"https://oxfamilibrary.openrepository.com/bitstream/handle/10546/621020/bk-narrative-power-collective-action-part1-080720-en.pdf;jsessionid=9DC8643C58F33DADDE5FC5520A326D54?sequence=7","x":4540,"y":-560,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"10f98b88cba4ec56","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sAxL_Zep2D0&ab_channel=FreudMuseumLondon","x":3860,"y":-540,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"d3ae47e0842336d2","type":"text","text":"Information processing theory posits that humans make decisions by systematically analyzing available data, akin to a computer processing inputs. This model assumes rational decision-making based on comprehensive information analysis. In contrast, the narrative patterns theory argues that humans primarily rely on pattern recognition and matching, typically scanning only 5-10% of available information and matching it against patterns stored in long-term memory. This approach emphasizes the role of past experiences, cultural context, and storytelling in shaping our understanding and decision-making processes.\n\nJerome Bruner's theory of cognitive modes offers a nuanced perspective that bridges these two approaches. Bruner proposed two complementary modes of thought: the paradigmatic (or logico-scientific) mode and the narrative mode. The paradigmatic mode aligns closely with the information processing theory, involving formal, mathematical systems of description and explanation. It deals with general causes, empirical truths, and uses procedures to establish verifiable references. The narrative mode, on the other hand, corresponds to the narrative patterns theory, focusing on human intentions, actions, and the consequences that mark their course. It places experiences in specific times and places and deals with the particulars of experience.\n\nWhile Bruner's narrative mode aligns more closely with how humans naturally make decisions and understand the world, he maintained that both modes are essential for human cognition. The paradigmatic mode, though less intuitive, plays a crucial role in creating and organizing knowledge. However, when it comes to day-to-day decision-making and action, humans tend to rely more heavily on the narrative mode.\n\nThe climate change issue exemplifies this dichotomy. While scientific data and logical arguments (paradigmatic mode) clearly demonstrate the reality and risks of climate change, many people's actions and beliefs are more influenced by narratives, personal experiences, and cultural stories about the environment and economy (narrative mode). This disconnect between available information and action highlights the predominance of narrative thinking in guiding behavior, even when logical evidence points in a different direction.\n\nIn conclusion, while both modes of thinking are valuable and used by humans, the narrative mode tends to dominate in decision-making and action, even as the paradigmatic mode contributes significantly to our creation and organization of knowledge.","x":620,"y":1300,"width":500,"height":1440},
		{"id":"808a47f3d9f18d96","type":"text","text":"Narratives are important because:\n1. Fundamental to human cognition: Humans are inherently \"homo narrans\" - storytelling creatures. Narratives help create and form patterns that are crucial for human decision-making.\n\t1. \"Our considerable capacity to utilise and blend patterns is the basis of our intelligence, and evolutionary adaptability is more associated with pattern utilisation than information processing.\"\n\n3. Natural form of communication: Narratives are the principal mechanism of learning and knowledge transfer in groups.\n4.  Identity formation: Stories play a crucial role in forming and maintaining individual and group identities.\n5. Memory and recall: Narrative forms are more memorable and easier to recall than abstract information.\n6. Cultural transmission: Stories are a key mechanism for passing on knowledge, values, and cultural information.\n7. Tacit knowledge capture: Narratives can express tacit knowledge that is difficult to articulate in other forms.\n\n10. Complexity representation: Stories can effectively capture and convey the complexity of real-world situations.\n\t1. Non-linear representation: Narratives can effectively represent non-linear processes and unexpected outcomes.\n\t2. Context provision: Stories provide rich context that is often lost in more abstract forms of information.\n\t3. Ambiguity handling: Narratives can effectively represent and deal with ambiguous or complex situations.\n\t4. \"Stories carry with them ambiguity and their meaning can be interpreted in different ways in different contexts.\"\n\t5. Multiple perspectives: Stories can capture and convey multiple viewpoints and interpretations.\n\t6. Fractal nature: Stories are fractal, allowing for understanding at multiple levels of detail.\n\t7. \"Narratives are useful for complexity because there are no hypotheses in complexity research; instead there are historical, technical, and simulation analyses of processes over time that result in unexpected outcomes.\"\n\n11. Serendipitous discovery: Narrative approaches allow for unexpected insights and connections to emerge.\n\n12. Weak signal detection: Stories can reveal subtle cues and weak signals that might be missed in more structured data.\n\n13. Collective intelligence: Narrative approaches can tap into collective wisdom and experiences.\n\n14. Adaptability: Stories can be easily adapted and reinterpreted in different contexts.\n\n15. Innovation support: Narrative methods can support innovation by allowing for novel connections and ideas.\n\n16. Decision-making support: Stories provide a richer basis for decision-making than abstract data alone.\n17. Emotional engagement: Narratives engage emotions, making information more impactful and meaningful.\n\n","x":2260,"y":1300,"width":460,"height":2220},
		{"id":"72f07dc533ec2535","type":"text","text":"## Characteristics of Narratives\n> *\"If we think about the form and manner of human exchange then it is also important  \nto distinguish between story telling, in the sense of creating a structured narrative  \nwith a purpose, and the fragmented anecdotal exchange which characterises a  \nhuman conversation. In several years of work in this field, having gathered large  \nvolumes of narrative material, the vast bulk of original material if transcribed is  \nrarely more than a paragraph or two in length. Another key aspect of fragmented  \nanecdotal material is namely its ability to provide context to relevant material over  \nlonger periods of time. The act of abstracting original material into a structured  \ndocument or case study takes place in the context of time and place. As those  \nchange the relevance of the material will reduce. Abstraction means making choices  \nabout what is relevant and the level of understanding in the target audience. As a  \nresult there is inevitable loss in the process. The price of codification is abstraction,  \nand thereby loss; the value is in rapid diffusion (Boisot 1998). Excessive levels of  \nabstraction (for example to single words or phrases) would result in incoherence and  \nlittle value, but there is a sweet spot in which the material retains value over longer  \nperiods of time. This also entails recognising that a degree of ambiguity is essential.\"* [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\nThat naturally occurring stories come as fragmented anecdotes. Occasionally you  \nget a fully formed and developed story, but mostly they are anecdotal, often only a  \nparagraph long when transcribed. Those with most meaning are often the worse  \nconstructed. In one recent case, looking at the stories of school children on  \nleaving a secondary school in Singapore, the most powerful were from the least  \narticulate; there was less disguise. Paintings and pictures were also found often a  \nbetter form of narrative expression than a pure story in textual form.  [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\n> Faced with a difficult or intractable problem we are unlikely to look up best practice as a structured document. Instead we seek out people and other sources, for example the internet, gathering fragmented material that we select and blend with our own experience and the current context to determine how to act. [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\nA story is always told in a context, from a context. If you read it then it will  \ntrigger a reaction but the reaction is not necessary sympathetic to that intended or  \nexperienced by the story teller. Each reader has their own context and situation.  \nWe also take into account that anecdotes need to be captured in their native  \nlanguage. There needs to be some common context for any translation to be effective...  \nwe determined that the best way to achieve this was for the researcher to create a  \ntagging system of sufficient simplicity to be understood without active  \ninterpretation, and for the story teller to tag their own story [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\nHow narratives support abduction","x":2780,"y":2180,"width":700,"height":1200},
		{"id":"3707fb69d1167521","type":"file","file":"Do Not Publish/Drafts/Narratives.md","x":4050,"y":2000,"width":700,"height":8080},
		{"id":"61b237d64d38e9be","type":"text","text":"### Decision-Making\nIn the past, people have thought humans make decisions through information processing, by systematically analyzing available data, like a computer processing inputs. However, humans primarily rely on pattern recognition and matching, typically scanning only 5-10% of available information and matching it against patterns stored in long-term memory. \n\n> *\"A classic experiment created a video with six students, half dressed in white, half in black, who pass two basketballs between them. The viewer is instructed to count the number of times those dressed in white pass the basketball. During the short sequence that follows a student dressed in a gorilla suit crosses the screen, beats her chest and exists stage left. On completion of the exercise observers offer ranges of answers as to the number of passes, few if any see the gorilla.\"* [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\n%% how does the example above prove the point about pattern processing? %%\n\nWhen a firefighter captain decides to evacuate a burning building moments before it collapses, or when an emergency room doctor immediately recognizes a rare condition amid confusing symptoms, they're not following a decision tree or weighing pros and cons. Instead, they're engaging in what Gary Klein and other researchers in Naturalistic Decision Making (NDM) have identified as a sophisticated form of pattern recognition and story-building.\n\nInstead of comparing multiple options, experienced decision-makers:\n- Recognize patterns from past experiences\n- Construct stories to understand the current situation\n- Mentally simulate potential actions and their consequences\n- Act based on the first workable solution they identify\n\n> *The original use of narrative was a source for mapping knowledge... it arose from the deeply practical need to create a rich context from which it was possible to extract decisions and judgments to ask questions about knowledge in use (Snowden 1999). Narrative here was also shown to be a better recall mechanism for hidden knowledge than questions.* [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\nConsider this real example from Klein's research: A firefighter commander arrived at a house fire that seemed routine. However, something felt \"off\" about how the fire was burning. Based on subtle cues that didn't fit his usual pattern recognition, he ordered his team out. Moments later, the floor collapsed - the fire had been in the basement, not the ground floor as initially appeared. The commander couldn't articulate exactly what triggered his decision at the moment, but by building a story about what might be happening based on pattern recognition, he saved his team's lives.","x":5440,"y":3260,"width":580,"height":1240},
		{"id":"45b92114d3c360a1","type":"text","text":"what is the objective of this piece?\nto explore how narratives might help us navigate as a human complex system within the complex environment that surrounds us.\n\n","x":4800,"y":2060,"width":250,"height":300},
		{"id":"96bec72bf1486371","type":"text","text":"Something about how we create who we are by interpreting our history, so we can create our selves in different ways depending on the narrative (I saw a great Substack note about this I believe) \n","x":4788,"y":2420,"width":275,"height":240},
		{"id":"c345a33da9aa98fc","type":"link","url":"https://kurzman.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/1410/2011/06/Kurzman_Meaning-Making.pdf","x":3340,"y":820,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"5ee78f7b3336ad72","type":"file","file":"Images/Pasted image 20241025142856.png","x":5670,"y":1680,"width":400,"height":320},
		{"id":"5d973a6274e57f20","type":"text","text":"https://thecynefin.co/looking-back-at-the-covid-masssense-part-2/","x":5705,"y":2000,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"266f582f60354a9c","type":"file","file":"Images/Pasted image 20241025142919.png","x":6120,"y":1680,"width":571,"height":320},
		{"id":"dd969d6671bbcd14","type":"text","text":"https://thecynefin.co/patterns-of-change-conflict-3-of-3/","x":6120,"y":2000,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"2fb4645784cc07b8","type":"text","text":"## Narrative Transmission\nAnthropologist Clifford Geertz's work demonstrates how cultures use narratives to maintain and transmit complex social knowledge. These \"thick descriptions\" help people understand not just what happened, but the web of meanings and implications surrounding events.\n\nAt its core, thick description recognizes that human actions cannot be understood in isolation - they exist within complex webs of meaning that give them significance. Consider a simple head nod: in one context, it might signal agreement; in another, respect; in yet another, ironic dismissal. The physical action remains the same, but its meaning shifts dramatically depending on the cultural context, relationship between participants, and specific situation.\n\nCultural narratives serve as sophisticated knowledge management systems that preserve and transmit this type of complex understanding. Consider how indigenous Australian songlines operate - these are not simply stories or songs, but intricate knowledge systems that encode information about navigation across vast distances, location of water sources, safe and dangerous areas, proper social behavior, historical events, spiritual significance of places, and more. The genius of narrative systems is their ability to embed multiple layers of knowledge within memorable, transmissible forms. \n\nConsider how a senior surgeon develops expertise - while technical knowledge can be written in manuals, the more subtle aspects of surgical judgment come from stories shared in operating rooms and corridors: \"I had a case like this once...\" These narratives transmit crucial knowledge such as recognizing subtle patterns, managing unexpected complications, balancing competing priorities, and handling uncertainty and pressure.\n\nThese narratives work as knowledge systems because they preserve context, and convey tacit knowledge that's difficult to codify in formal procedures or documents. A business case study might provide facts about what happened, but a rich narrative shared by someone who lived through an event captures the ambiguity, emotion, and complexity of the actual situation. It preserves not just what happened, but how it felt, what wasn't known at the time, what might have happened but didn't, and why certain choices were made.\n\n%% something about the wider cultural value? eg. it helps unite around common identities. moral aspects %%","x":5440,"y":4540,"width":580,"height":1040},
		{"id":"b5d942fe91448c9f","type":"text","text":"### Conceptual Blending\n> *\"Our ability to link and blend patterns in unusual ways, known as conceptual blending (Fauconnier & Turner 2002), gives us ability to adapt rapidly to changing context and critically to innovate as well as to use that most powerful tool of explanation, knowledge transfer and teaching, **metaphor**.\"* [Narrative research](https://narrate.typepad.com/100816-narrative-research_snowden-final.pdf)\n\nTo understand how conceptual blending works, consider the statement \"This surgeon is a butcher.\" When we process this statement, we bring together two distinct mental spaces: that of surgery and that of butchery. The surgical space contains elements like operating rooms, precision instruments, healing intentions, and years of medical training. The butcher's space involves cutting meat, using cleavers and saws, preparing food, and working with dead tissue. The resulting meaning isn't simply a comparison of surgeons and butchers - instead, it creates an entirely new concept that suggests incompetence through the mismatch between surgical precision and butcher's methods.\n\nConceptual blending proves particularly powerful in scientific understanding. Consider how we think about information flow in organizations or computer systems. This concept blends our understanding of physical flow (like water in pipes or traffic on roads) with abstract concepts about information and communication. The resulting blend helps us understand and work with information in new ways, creating concepts like bandwidth, bottlenecks, and channels that aren't literally true but provide powerful tools for thinking and problem-solving.\n\nRather than seeing innovation as simply combining existing elements, we can understand it as a process of creating new mental spaces that generate their own properties and insights. Narratives play a crucial role in enabling conceptual blending because they can hold multiple mental spaces simultaneously while showing how they interact.","x":6020,"y":3260,"width":640,"height":800},
		{"id":"fa9554160eb2da80","type":"text","text":"> *“'The primary purpose of narrative,' media scholar Katherine Hayles argued several years ago, 'is to search for meaning,' which makes narrative an essential technology for human beings, who can arguably be defined as meaning-­seeking animals...' Narrative is a tool by which we extract (or impose, depending on your perspective) meaning from the chaotic flux of being in the world”* [Narrative Collapse](https://theconvivialsociety.substack.com/p/narrative-collapse)","x":4800,"y":3820,"width":400,"height":300},
		{"id":"3f8244195ec50358","type":"text","text":"## Narratives in Complexity\nUnderstanding thick description and narratives as knowledge systems helps us recognize why stories are so crucial for managing complexity. They don't just transmit isolated facts - they maintain entire systems of meaning that help people navigate complex situations. \n\n> *\"[A] good narrative is a complex one and that complexity is best understood with a narrative... [T]he complexity and ambiguity of the environments that individuals face are best understood when language, including the richness of metaphor and the flexibility of the story, is invoked as a sensemaking device... Stories can complexify meanings in a way that linguistic statements cannot (Snowden, 1999).\"*  [The use of narrative to understand and respond to complexity](https://thecynefin.co/wp-content/uploads/2005/01/51-Browning-Boudes-on-Weick-and-Snowden.pdf?srsltid=AfmBOoq5JvyRFhScGslHyMulRDUvmt0EgqKWbNkCDHKCHdosxzmV4FU1)\n\n[[Complex Systems]] include situations or environments that are hard to predict or control because many different factors are involved that interact with each other in unexpected ways. There are no clear cause-and-effect relationships, and what worked before might not work again. Rather than reduce situations to simple cause-and-effect relationships, narratives can capture emergent patterns and non-linear relationships without oversimplifying them. \n\n>*\"Complex environments must be matched with equally complex processing mechanisms. The capacity of the narrative to vary in punctuation (when they begin and end), pace (what is the speed and variation between sequences), and participant composition (casts can range from one person, to few, to ensembles) means the narrative is a communicative form that is frequently consistent with organizational complexity (Luhman & Boje, 2001; Polster, 1987).\"* [The use of narrative to understand and respond to complexity](https://thecynefin.co/wp-content/uploads/2005/01/51-Browning-Boudes-on-Weick-and-Snowden.pdf?srsltid=AfmBOoq5JvyRFhScGslHyMulRDUvmt0EgqKWbNkCDHKCHdosxzmV4FU1)\n\nNarratives can effectively capture and convey the complexity of real-world situations through:\n2. **Context provision:** Stories provide rich context that is often lost in more abstract forms of information. \"experts’ language is so restricted and [abstract](https://thecynefin.co/wp-content/uploads/2005/01/51-Browning-Boudes-on-Weick-and-Snowden.pdf?srsltid=AfmBOoq5JvyRFhScGslHyMulRDUvmt0EgqKWbNkCDHKCHdosxzmV4FU1) that it too easily remains about the problem, but far above it.\"\n4. **Ambiguity handling:** Narratives can effectively represent and deal with non-linear processes and ambiguous situations, \"and their meaning can be interpreted in different ways in different contexts.\" Edgar Morin, who argues that narratives allow us to hold contradictory ideas simultaneously and understand how they interact - something particularly valuable in complex systems where paradoxes often exist.\n5. Multiple perspectives: Stories can capture and convey multiple viewpoints and interpretations. From the first text's discussion of polyvocality: \"Diverse information causes a person not only to see different information but also to see information differently.\"\n7. Unexpected outcomes: Narrative approaches allow for unexpected insights and connections to emerge. \"Narratives are useful for complexity because there are no hypotheses in complexity research; instead there are historical, technical, and simulation analyses of processes over time that result in unexpected outcomes.\" ([[Narrative research - Dave Snowden]]). Stories can reveal subtle cues and weak signals that might be missed in more structured data. ([[Narrative research - Dave Snowden]])","x":2860,"y":3620,"width":640,"height":1400},
		{"id":"7f0819c383b70cb6","type":"text","text":"## Narratives & Sensemaking\n> *\"[T]he complexity and ambiguity of the environments that individuals face are best understood when language, including the richness of metaphor and the flexibility of the story, is invoked as a sensemaking device... Stories can complexify meanings in a way that linguistic statements cannot (Snowden, 1999).\"*  [The use of narrative to understand and respond to complexity](https://thecynefin.co/wp-content/uploads/2005/01/51-Browning-Boudes-on-Weick-and-Snowden.pdf?srsltid=AfmBOoq5JvyRFhScGslHyMulRDUvmt0EgqKWbNkCDHKCHdosxzmV4FU1)\n### Context\nStories provide rich context that is often lost in more abstract forms of information.\n\n> *\"Narrative is our default sense-making technique, in part, because it reflects our fundamentally time-bound existence. We experience life as a succession of moments yielding a discernible past, present, and future... They are selective and purposeful: events are included so as to yield or imply meaningful relationships, establishing not only what has happened but also why and with what significance.\"* [Narrative Collapse](https://theconvivialsociety.substack.com/p/narrative-collapse)\n\n>*\"The capacity of the narrative to vary in punctuation (when they begin and end), pace (what is the speed and variation between sequences), and participant composition (casts can range from one person, to few, to ensembles) means the narrative is a communicative form that is frequently consistent with organizational complexity (Luhman & Boje, 2001; Polster, 1987).\"* [The use of narrative to understand and respond to complexity](https://thecynefin.co/wp-content/uploads/2005/01/51-Browning-Boudes-on-Weick-and-Snowden.pdf?srsltid=AfmBOoq5JvyRFhScGslHyMulRDUvmt0EgqKWbNkCDHKCHdosxzmV4FU1)\n### Ambiguity\nNarratives can effectively represent and deal with non-linear processes and ambiguous situations, \"and their meaning can be interpreted in different ways in different contexts.\" Edgar Morin, who argues that narratives allow us to hold contradictory ideas simultaneously and understand how they interact - something particularly valuable in complex systems where paradoxes often exist.\n\nStories can capture and convey multiple viewpoints and interpretations. From the first text's discussion of polyvocality: \"Diverse information causes a person not only to see different information but also to see information differently.\"\n\n> *\"no story is ever truly linear because our relationship to any story cannot be linear. as the audience we can stop, pause, replay, reread, skip, leave, look away, come back. we are always acting agents, and this interactivity is integral to the storytelling experience.\"*  [Storytelling as Gift, Storytelling as Currency](https://www.are.na/editorial/storytelling-as-gift-storytelling-as-currency)\n\n> *\"storytelling reveals meaning without committing the error of defining it…”* [Storytelling as Gift, Storytelling as Currency](https://www.are.na/editorial/storytelling-as-gift-storytelling-as-currency)\n### Directionality\nStories can reveal subtle cues and weak signals that might be missed in more structured data. ([[Narrative research - Dave Snowden]])\n\nStories as an attractor\n","x":3500,"y":3860,"width":440,"height":1940},
		{"id":"9ef6c752864567a3","type":"file","file":"Images/Pasted image from Storytelling as Gift, Storytelling as Currency.png","x":2920,"y":5400,"width":381,"height":400},
		{"id":"63c5762e57b2a52c","type":"text","text":"They work by preserving context, and conveying tacit knowledge that's difficult to codify in formal procedures or documents. ","x":4800,"y":4160,"width":250,"height":160},
		{"id":"f23ffd44d807ce18","type":"text","text":"> *\"We never merely perceive the world, we interpret it. In fact, our perception is already interpretation. And the work of interpretation depends to no small degree on the stories that we have internalized about the world.\"* [Narrative Collapse](https://theconvivialsociety.substack.com/p/narrative-collapse)\n\n","x":4750,"y":4830,"width":370,"height":230},
		{"id":"875196b610e5d53c","type":"text","text":"\n> *\"When Byung-Chul discusses Narrative, by contrast, he generally means a story that defines some “closed order” of being (ix). So for Christians, in Byung-Chul’s sense, the Biblical narrative is a closed order, in that we know where we come from, we hope we know where we’re going, and we know 'what it all means' at some basic level, even if some items such as the problem of evil remain thorny. So capital “N” narratives provide meaning, they orient us in some particular non-consumer direction in life, they close off some possibilities while prescribing others, and so on.\"* [Rendering Me into We: A Review of The Crisis of Narration](https://www.frontporchrepublic.com/2024/05/rendering-me-into-we-a-review-of-the-crisis-of-narration/)\n\n\n> *\"Group behaviour can be understood as attempts to change the world according to a specific collectively shared narrative about social reality (Bliuc, Smith, & Moynihan, [2020](https://compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/spc3.12670#spc312670-bib-0014)).\"* [From cooperation to conflict](https://compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/spc3.12670)\n","x":4840,"y":5200,"width":460,"height":480}
	],
	"edges":[]
}